meta {
  name: Set Chart Defaults
  type: http
  seq: 24
}

put {
  url: {{protocol}}://{{host}}:{{port}}/{{apiPrefix}}analytes/{{temperatureAnalyteId}}/chart-defaults
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

body:json {
  {
    "overlays": [
      { "kind": "medication_tag", "value": "antipyretic" }
    ]
  }
}

script:pre-request {
  if (!bru.getVar('temperatureAnalyteId')) {
    await bru.runRequest("analytes/list-vitals");
  }
}

settings {
  encodeUrl: true
  timeout: 0
}

docs {
  Replaces this metric's chart overlay defaults **whole** — the same edit-whole shape as a
  patient's `devices` list. The submitted array becomes the stored one, duplicate
  (`kind`, `value`) pairs collapse to a single row, and `{"overlays": []}` clears the metric's
  defaults, which is how a household opts a chart out of default markers entirely.

  `kind` accepts only `medication_tag` in Phase 1 (anything else is a 400). The shape deliberately
  admits more kinds later — `intervention_type`, e.g. dressing changes on a lesion-size chart — but
  a kind ships only once it has a render rule.

  `value` is matched **exactly** against `medications.tags`, the same rule
  `GET /timeline?medicationTag=` uses. A tag no medication currently carries is accepted rather
  than rejected: it matches nothing today and starts working the day a medication gains it.
  Conversely, retagging a medication re-classifies its past doses on every chart at once — that
  retroactivity is the point of keeping this as config rather than a field on each dose.

  Verify the effect with `Timeline → Get Timeline` (its `temperatureOverlayTags` mirrors what you
  set here) and with `Analytes → History` (its `doses` array carries the matching doses).
}
